---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";

const title = "About Online Sound Meter";
const description =
  "About the Online Sound Meter — a free, private, browser-based sound level meter, our measurement methodology, and the authoritative sources behind our guidance.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/about/">
  <Section as="h1" eyebrow="About" title="A better free sound meter">
    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        Online Sound Meter is a free tool for measuring environmental noise in
        decibels, built for teachers, parents, musicians, office workers and
        anyone curious about the sound around them. It runs entirely in your
        browser, works on any device, and never records or uploads your audio.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most free meters online show a single number with little context. We
        wanted something more useful and more honest — real measurement metrics
        (A/C/Z weighting, Fast/Slow response, LAeq), clear explanations of what
        the numbers mean, and a frank account of the limits of consumer
        hardware.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="methodology" class="display-tight mt-12 scroll-mt-20 text-2xl text-ink">
      Methodology
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        Sound is captured with the Web Audio API and analysed with a 4096-point
        FFT. We compute RMS amplitude, convert to decibels, apply IEC 61672
        frequency weightings and exponential time-weightings, and derive the
        equivalent continuous level (LAeq) from sound energy. The full pipeline
        is described on our
        <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="/how-it-works/"
          >how it works</a
        > page, and its limits on the
        <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="/accuracy/">accuracy</a> page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="display-tight mt-12 text-2xl text-ink">Sources</h2>
    <ul class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-3 text-sm text-ink-muted">
      <li>
        <a class="text-ink underline hover:text-primary" href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/topics/noise/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
          >NIOSH — Occupational Noise Exposure</a
        > · recommended exposure limits and the 3 dB exchange rate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="text-ink underline hover:text-primary" href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/deafness-and-hearing-loss" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
          >WHO — Deafness and hearing loss</a
        > · environmental and recreational noise guidance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a class="text-ink underline hover:text-primary" href="https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/health/noise-induced-hearing-loss" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank"
          >NIDCD — Noise-Induced Hearing Loss</a
        > · how loud sounds damage hearing.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</Layout>
